  "beruf": "gastronomie",
  "title": "Hospitality: cafés, restaurants and kitchens",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "portuguese-for-work run, 2026-11-22: written by hand for Portugal (European Portuguese, pt-PT), not yet read by a human. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon, the IMT deck or another sector are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions, contracts and rules are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "Cafés, restaurants and snack bars (a restauração) employ many newcomers in Portugal, in the kitchen and on the floor. This deck has the kitchen and its equipment, the tasks, taking orders and serving, hygiene and safety, and what you say to guests and colleagues. In the kitchen people say tu; guests you don't know get the third person, often with no pronoun: «O que deseja?», «Querem ver a ementa?»",
  "introEasy": "Do you work in a café or restaurant in Portugal? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Empregado/a de mesa",
    "Empregado/a de balcão",
    "Ajudante de cozinha",
    "Copeiro/a",
    "Cozinheiro/a",
    "Pasteleiro/a"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "Café orders are short: «Uma bica e um pastel de nata, se faz favor.» Learn the coffee words early: uma bica or um café (espresso; um cimbalino in Porto), um galão (milky coffee in a glass), uma meia de leite.",
    "Repeat the order back so nothing goes wrong: «Então, são dois pratos do dia e uma salada, certo?»",
    "Guests ask for the bill with «A conta, se faz favor». Many ask for a receipt with their tax number: «Quer contribuinte na fatura?» means: do you want your NIF on the receipt?",
    "Food hygiene rules (HACCP) apply to every kitchen: wash your hands, label food with the date, keep raw and cooked food apart. Ask your boss to show you the kitchen's plan. Training and medical checks for food handlers depend on the employer: ask. General orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
